      language: 'English',
      instructor_name: 'Smart21Brain',
      certificate_enabled: true,
      thumbnail_url: IMG('photo-1614064641938-3bbee52942c7'),
      objectives: ["Spot a real customer problem and test an idea cheaply", "Research customers and competitors", "Calculate costs, prices, profit and break-even", "Keep simple records and write a one-page business plan"],
      requirements: ["None"],
      lessons: lessons("business-and-entrepreneurship-essentials", [
        "Module 1: Start with the customer",
        ["From idea to opportunity", "text", 600, "## Good businesses solve problems\nThe best ideas begin with a **real problem** that people already have and will pay to fix.\n\n{{flow Spot a problem > Think of a solution > Check that people want it > Test it small > Improve and grow}}\n\n## Three quick questions\n1. Is the problem real and common?\n2. Will people pay for the solution?\n3. Can I deliver it with the money and skills I have?\n\n> Example: Students near a college have no cheap, quick lunch. A seller offers a TZS 2,000 lunch box. The problem is real, the price is affordable and the owner can cook.\n\n> Tip: Test small first. Sell to ten customers before you rent a shop.\n\n> Try it: Name one problem in your neighbourhood that someone could solve with a small business. || Answers vary", {"title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"prompt": "A good business idea usually starts with...", "options": ["A fancy logo", "A real customer problem", "A big loan", "Copying a rival exactly"], "answer": 1, "why": "Solve a problem people will pay for."}, {"prompt": "Why test an idea on a small scale first?", "options": ["It looks professional", "It limits risk and cost", "It avoids paying tax", "It removes competition"], "answer": 1, "why": "Small tests are cheap and teach you quickly."}, {"prompt": "Which question checks demand?", "options": ["Do I like the colour?", "Will people pay for it?", "Is it new in the world?", "Can I advertise on TV?"], "answer": 1, "why": "Paying customers prove demand."}]}],
        ["Know your customers and competitors", "text", 600, "## Market research\nFind out who your customers are and what they want before you spend money.\n\n| Method | How it works | Good for |\n|---|---|---|\n| Survey | Short questions to many people | Numbers and trends |\n| Interview | Detailed chat with a few people | Reasons and feelings |\n| Observation | Watch how people buy | Real behaviour |\n\n## Target market\nYou cannot serve everyone. Describe your **target customer**: who they are, where they live, what they can afford.\n\n## Competitors\nList who else solves the same problem. Note their prices, strengths and weaknesses, then decide how you will be **different or better**.\n\n> Try it: Name two ways to learn what customers want. || e.g. interviews and observation", {"title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"prompt": "Your target market is...", "options": ["Everyone in the country", "The group of customers you aim to serve", "Your suppliers", "Your employees"], "answer": 1, "why": "Focus on one group first."}, {"prompt": "The best way to learn what customers want is to...", "options": ["Guess", "Ask and observe them", "Copy a rival's prices", "Wait for sales"], "answer": 1, "why": "Interviews, surveys and observation."}, {"prompt": "Studying competitors helps you to...", "options": ["Break the law", "Decide how to be different or better", "Avoid customers", "Stop selling"], "answer": 1, "why": "Know the market you enter."}]}],
        "Module 2: Money and planning",
        ["Costs, pricing and profit", "text", 720, "## Two kinds of cost\n- **Fixed costs** stay the same however much you sell (rent, licence).\n- **Variable costs** rise with each item sold (ingredients, packaging).\n\n## Key formulas\n| Idea | Formula |\n|---|---|\n| Profit | Revenue − total costs |\n| Break-even (units) | Fixed costs ÷ (price − variable cost per unit) |\n\n> Example: A soap maker pays TZS 60,000 rent each month. Each soap costs TZS 1,000 to make and sells for TZS 2,500. Break-even = 60,000 ÷ (2,500 − 1,000) = **40 soaps** a month.\n\n> Try it: Revenue is TZS 500,000 and total costs are TZS 380,000. What is the profit? || TZS 120,000", {"title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"prompt": "Shop rent is an example of a...", "options": ["Variable cost", "Fixed cost", "Revenue", "Profit"], "answer": 1, "why": "Rent does not change with sales."}, {"prompt": "Revenue TZS 500,000 and costs TZS 380,000 give a profit of...", "options": ["TZS 80,000", "TZS 120,000", "TZS 880,000", "TZS 380,000"], "answer": 1, "why": "500,000 − 380,000."}, {"prompt": "Fixed costs TZS 30,000, price TZS 1,500, variable cost TZS 500 per unit. Break-even is...", "options": ["20 units", "30 units", "60 units", "15 units"], "answer": 1, "why": "30,000 ÷ (1,500 − 500) = 30."}]}],
        ["Records, cash and your one-page plan", "text", 720, "## Keep simple records\n- Write every sale and expense in a **cash book**.\n- Keep receipts.\n- **Separate** business money from personal money.\n\n> Tip: A business can be profitable on paper and still run out of cash if customers pay late. Watch your **cash flow**.\n\n## A one-page business plan\n| Section | Answer this |\n|---|---|\n| Problem | What problem do you solve? |\n| Solution | What do you sell? |\n| Customers | Who will buy? |\n| Competitors | Who else sells this? |\n| Money | Prices, costs, expected profit |\n| First steps | What will you do in the next 30 days? |\n\n> Try it: Why should personal and business money be kept separate? || So you can see the real profit of the business", {"title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"prompt": "A cash book records...", "options": ["Only profits", "Money coming in and going out", "Customer names only", "Staff holidays"], "answer": 1, "why": "Every sale and expense."}, {"prompt": "Why separate personal and business money?", "options": ["To pay less tax", "To see the true profit of the business", "Because banks insist", "It is not needed"], "answer": 1, "why": "Mixing hides real performance."}, {"prompt": "A business plan should state...", "options": ["Only the logo", "The problem, customers, money and first steps", "Only the name", "Only the loan amount"], "answer": 1, "why": "A plan covers the whole idea."}]}],
      ]),
