      lessons: lessons("office-and-digital-skills", [
        "Module 1: Documents and numbers",
        ["Word processing essentials", "text", 600, "## A document people enjoy reading\n- Use **Heading styles** (Heading 1, 2, 3) instead of just making text bigger. They create structure and an automatic table of contents.\n- Keep **line spacing** and margins consistent.\n- Add **page numbers**.\n- Use **Save as PDF** to share a document so the layout cannot change.\n\n## Shortcuts that save time\n| Shortcut | Action |\n|---|---|\n| Ctrl + C / V / X | Copy / paste / cut |\n| Ctrl + Z | Undo |\n| Ctrl + B | Bold |\n| Ctrl + S | Save |\n\n> Try it: Which shortcut undoes your last action? || Ctrl + Z", {"title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"prompt": "Why use Heading styles?", "options": ["They look random", "They build structure and a table of contents", "They shrink the file", "They remove spelling errors"], "answer": 1, "why": "Styles give real document structure."}, {"prompt": "Which shortcut saves a document?", "options": ["Ctrl + S", "Ctrl + B", "Ctrl + X", "Ctrl + P"], "answer": 0, "why": "S for Save."}, {"prompt": "The best format to share a document so the layout stays fixed is...", "options": ["PDF", "Plain text", "Image of a screen", "Spreadsheet"], "answer": 0, "why": "PDF keeps the layout."}]}],
        ["Spreadsheet basics and formulas", "text", 660, "## Cells, rows and columns\nA spreadsheet is a grid. Each cell has an address such as **B3** (column B, row 3).\n\n## Formulas always start with =\n| Formula | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| =B2+B3 | Adds two cells |\n| =SUM(B2:B6) | Adds a whole range |\n| =AVERAGE(B2:B6) | Finds the mean |\n| =MAX(B2:B6) | Finds the largest |\n\n> Example: A monthly budget lists expenses in B2 to B6. =SUM(B2:B6) gives the total and updates automatically if you change a number.\n\n> Try it: Which formula adds the numbers from C1 to C10? || =SUM(C1:C10)", {"title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"prompt": "Every formula in a spreadsheet starts with...", "options": ["#", "=", "@", "$"], "answer": 1, "why": "The equals sign."}, {"prompt": "=SUM(B2:B6) will...", "options": ["Multiply the cells", "Add cells B2 to B6", "Count words", "Delete the cells"], "answer": 1, "why": "SUM adds a range."}, {"prompt": "The cell in column C, row 4 is...", "options": ["4C", "C4", "C-4", "CC4"], "answer": 1, "why": "Column letter first, then the row number."}]}],
        "Module 2: Presenting and communicating",
        ["Presentations that work", "text", 540, "## Slides support you, they do not replace you\n- **One idea per slide.**\n- Keep text short: a few lines, not paragraphs.\n- Use large, readable fonts and strong contrast.\n- Choose clear pictures or charts instead of decoration.\n- **Practise** out loud and check your timing.\n\n{{flow Know your audience > Plan 3 main points > Design simple slides > Rehearse > Present with eye contact}}\n\n> Try it: Why is a slide with a full paragraph a poor choice? || The audience reads instead of listening to you", {"title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"prompt": "A good slide has...", "options": ["A full page of text", "One main idea", "Five different fonts", "No heading"], "answer": 1, "why": "Keep it simple."}, {"prompt": "The best way to prepare is to...", "options": ["Read the slides word for word", "Rehearse out loud", "Skip the practice", "Copy another deck"], "answer": 1, "why": "Practice builds confidence and timing."}, {"prompt": "Strong text contrast means...", "options": ["Light text on a light background", "Dark text on a light background or the reverse", "Using many colours", "Small letters"], "answer": 1, "why": "It makes slides readable."}]}],
        ["Professional email and smart online research", "text", 600, "## Writing a good email\n- Use a clear **subject line**.\n- Greet politely, state your purpose in the first lines, then thank the reader.\n- Check attachments before sending.\n- **CC** shows others the message; **BCC** hides their addresses.\n\n## Judging a website\nAsk: **Who wrote it? When? What evidence is given? Why was it published?**\n\n## Stay safe\nUse a different strong password for each important account and never share it.\n\n> Try it: When should you use BCC? || When you do not want recipients to see each other's addresses", {"title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"prompt": "A subject line should be...", "options": ["Left empty", "Clear and specific", "In capital letters", "A long story"], "answer": 1, "why": "It tells the reader what the email is about."}, {"prompt": "BCC is used to...", "options": ["Hide recipients' addresses from each other", "Delete the email", "Add a signature", "Encrypt the file"], "answer": 0, "why": "Blind carbon copy."}, {"prompt": "To judge a website you should check the...", "options": ["Colour scheme", "Author, date and evidence", "Number of ads", "Font size"], "answer": 1, "why": "Credibility comes from author and evidence."}]}],
      ]),
