      thumbnail_url: IMG('photo-1677442136019-21780ecad995'),
      objectives: ["Calculate mean, median, mode, range and standard deviation", "Use the rules of probability", "Work with the normal distribution and z-scores", "Explain the logic of a hypothesis test"],
      requirements: ["O-Level or A-Level mathematics"],
      lessons: lessons("statistics-for-university-students", [
        "Module 1: Data and probability",
        ["Describing data", "text", 720, "## Measures of centre\n- **Mean** = sum ÷ number of values\n- **Median** = middle value when sorted\n- **Mode** = most frequent value\n\n## Measures of spread\n- **Range** = largest − smallest\n- **Standard deviation (s)** = typical distance from the mean\n\n> Example: Data: 4, 6, 6, 8, 11. Mean = 35 ÷ 5 = **7**. Median = **6**. Mode = **6**. Range = **7**.\n\n> Example: Sample standard deviation. Differences from the mean: −3, −1, −1, 1, 4. Squares: 9, 1, 1, 1, 16, total 28. Divide by (n − 1) = 4 to get 7, then take the square root: s = √7 ≈ **2.65**.\n\n> Try it: What is the median of 3, 9, 5, 7, 1? || 5", {"title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"prompt": "The median of 3, 9, 5, 7, 1 is...", "options": ["3", "5", "7", "9"], "answer": 1, "why": "Sorted: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9. The middle is 5."}, {"prompt": "The mean of 2, 4, 6, 8 is...", "options": ["4", "5", "6", "20"], "answer": 1, "why": "20 ÷ 4 = 5."}, {"prompt": "Standard deviation measures...", "options": ["The centre", "The spread of the data", "The total", "The largest value"], "answer": 1, "why": "It shows how spread out data are."}]}],
        ["Probability basics", "text", 660, "## Probability scale\nProbability runs from **0** (impossible) to **1** (certain).\n\n## **P(event) = favourable outcomes ÷ total outcomes**\n\n| Rule | Formula |\n|---|---|\n| Complement | P(not A) = 1 − P(A) |\n| Addition | P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B) |\n| Independent events | P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B) |\n\n> Example: Rolling an even number on a fair die: 3 ÷ 6 = **0.5**.\n\n> Example: Two fair coin tosses both land heads: 0.5 × 0.5 = **0.25**.\n\n> Try it: If P(rain) = 0.3, what is P(no rain)? || 0.7", {"title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"prompt": "P(even number) on a fair six-sided die is...", "options": ["1/6", "1/3", "1/2", "2/3"], "answer": 2, "why": "Three even numbers out of six."}, {"prompt": "If P(rain) = 0.3 then P(no rain) =", "options": ["0.3", "0.5", "0.7", "1.3"], "answer": 2, "why": "1 − 0.3."}, {"prompt": "For independent events with probabilities 0.5 and 0.4, P(both) =", "options": ["0.9", "0.2", "0.1", "0.45"], "answer": 1, "why": "0.5 × 0.4 = 0.2."}]}],
        "Module 2: Distributions and testing",
        ["The normal distribution", "text", 720, "## The bell curve\nMany measurements (heights, test marks) follow a **normal distribution**: symmetric, with mean, median and mode in the middle.\n\n## The 68-95-99.7 rule\n| Within | Share of data |\n|---|---|\n| 1 standard deviation | about 68% |\n| 2 standard deviations | about 95% |\n| 3 standard deviations | about 99.7% |\n\n## z-score\n## **z = (x − μ) ÷ σ**\nIt tells you how many standard deviations a value is from the mean.\n\n> Example: Marks have mean 60 and standard deviation 10. A mark of 80 has z = (80 − 60) ÷ 10 = **2**, higher than about 97.5% of students.\n\n> Try it: Find z for a mark of 45 (mean 60, standard deviation 10). || −1.5", {"title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"prompt": "About what share of data lies within 2 standard deviations of the mean?", "options": ["68%", "95%", "99.7%", "50%"], "answer": 1, "why": "The 95% rule."}, {"prompt": "With μ = 60 and σ = 10, the z-score of 45 is...", "options": ["1.5", "−1.5", "15", "−15"], "answer": 1, "why": "(45 − 60) ÷ 10."}, {"prompt": "A normal distribution is...", "options": ["Skewed to the right", "Symmetric around the mean", "Flat", "Always uniform"], "answer": 1, "why": "It is bell-shaped and symmetric."}]}],
        ["Hypothesis testing in plain language", "text", 720, "## The logic\nWe test a claim about a population using sample data.\n\n- **Null hypothesis (H₀):** nothing special is happening (no effect).\n- **Alternative hypothesis (H₁):** there is an effect.\n- **Significance level (α):** the risk of a false alarm you accept, often **0.05**.\n- **p-value:** how likely data this extreme would be **if H₀ were true**.\n\n{{flow State H0 and H1 > Choose alpha > Collect data and calculate p > Compare p with alpha > Decide}}\n\n**Decision rule:** if **p < α**, reject H₀. Otherwise do not reject it.\n\n| Error | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Type I | Rejecting H₀ when it is true (false positive) |\n| Type II | Not rejecting H₀ when it is false (missed effect) |\n\n> Try it: p = 0.03 and α = 0.05. What do you decide? || Reject H₀", {"title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"prompt": "If p = 0.03 and α = 0.05 you should...", "options": ["Reject H₀", "Accept H₀ as proven", "Collect no data", "Set α = 0.01 afterwards"], "answer": 0, "why": "p is smaller than α."}, {"prompt": "A Type I error is...", "options": ["Rejecting a true H₀", "Keeping a false H₀", "A calculation slip", "Having a large sample"], "answer": 0, "why": "A false positive."}, {"prompt": "The p-value is the probability of data at least this extreme...", "options": ["If H₀ is true", "If H₁ is true", "Always 0.05", "After you reject H₀"], "answer": 0, "why": "It assumes H₀ is true."}]}],
      ]),
