      thumbnail_url: IMG('photo-1524661135-423995f22d0b'),
      objectives: ["Explain scarcity and opportunity cost", "Use demand and supply to explain prices", "Calculate a market equilibrium", "Calculate and interpret price elasticity of demand"],
      requirements: ["Basic algebra"],
      lessons: lessons("microeconomics-fundamentals", [
        "Module 1: Choices and markets",
        ["Scarcity, choice and opportunity cost", "text", 540, "## The basic problem\nHuman **wants** are unlimited but **resources** (time, money, land, labour) are limited. This is **scarcity**, so we must choose.\n\n## Opportunity cost\nThe **opportunity cost** of a choice is the **next best alternative you give up**.\n\n> Example: You can study for 2 hours or work and earn TZS 3,000 an hour. If you study, your opportunity cost is **TZS 6,000**.\n\n> Tip: Opportunity cost is not always money. It can be time, comfort or a different opportunity.\n\n> Try it: A farmer uses a field for maize instead of beans. What is the opportunity cost? || The beans (income) that could have been grown", {"title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"prompt": "Scarcity means...", "options": ["There is no money", "Wants are unlimited but resources are limited", "Goods are free", "Prices are high"], "answer": 1, "why": "Limited resources, unlimited wants."}, {"prompt": "Opportunity cost is...", "options": ["The price paid", "The next best alternative given up", "The total cost", "A tax"], "answer": 1, "why": "What you sacrifice."}, {"prompt": "Studying 2 hours instead of earning TZS 3,000 per hour has an opportunity cost of...", "options": ["TZS 3,000", "TZS 6,000", "TZS 1,500", "Nothing"], "answer": 1, "why": "2 × 3,000."}]}],
        ["Demand and supply", "text", 660, "## Law of demand\nWhen price **rises**, quantity demanded **falls** (other things equal). The demand curve slopes **downward**.\n\n## Law of supply\nWhen price **rises**, quantity supplied **rises**. The supply curve slopes **upward**.\n\n## Moves and shifts\n- A change in the **good's own price** moves you **along** the curve.\n- A change in something else **shifts** the whole curve.\n\n| Shifts demand | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| Income | Richer buyers buy more of a normal good |\n| Tastes | A trend makes a product popular |\n| Price of related goods | A substitute becomes cheaper |\n| Population | More buyers |\n\n> Try it: A rise in consumers' income (normal good) shifts demand which way? || To the right (increase)", {"title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"prompt": "According to the law of demand, when price rises, quantity demanded...", "options": ["Rises", "Falls", "Stays fixed", "Doubles"], "answer": 1, "why": "They move in opposite directions."}, {"prompt": "A change in the good's own price causes...", "options": ["A shift of the curve", "A movement along the curve", "No change", "A new market"], "answer": 1, "why": "Own price = movement along."}, {"prompt": "Higher income for a normal good...", "options": ["Shifts demand right", "Shifts demand left", "Shifts supply left", "Does nothing"], "answer": 0, "why": "People buy more."}]}],
        "Module 2: Equilibrium and elasticity",
        ["Market equilibrium", "text", 660, "## Where the lines cross\n**Equilibrium** is the price where **quantity demanded = quantity supplied**.\n\n- Price **above** equilibrium: **surplus** pushes price down.\n- Price **below** equilibrium: **shortage** pushes price up.\n\n> Example: Qd = 100 − 2P and Qs = 20 + 2P. Set them equal: 100 − 2P = 20 + 2P, so 80 = 4P, **P = 20**. Then Q = 100 − 40 = **60**.\n\n> Tip: A price ceiling set **below** equilibrium creates a shortage.\n\n> Try it: At P = 30 in the example, is there a surplus or shortage? || Surplus (Qs = 80, Qd = 40)", {"title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"prompt": "At equilibrium...", "options": ["Qd = Qs", "Price is zero", "Qd > Qs", "Qs > Qd"], "answer": 0, "why": "Demand equals supply."}, {"prompt": "A price above equilibrium creates a...", "options": ["Shortage", "Surplus", "Tax", "Subsidy"], "answer": 1, "why": "Supply exceeds demand."}, {"prompt": "For Qd = 100 − 2P and Qs = 20 + 2P, the equilibrium price is...", "options": ["10", "20", "30", "40"], "answer": 1, "why": "100 − 2P = 20 + 2P gives P = 20."}]}],
        ["Price elasticity of demand", "text", 660, "## How sensitive are buyers?\n## **PED = % change in quantity demanded ÷ % change in price**\n\n| Value (ignoring sign) | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Greater than 1 | Elastic: buyers react strongly |\n| Less than 1 | Inelastic: buyers react weakly |\n| Equal to 1 | Unit elastic |\n\n> Example: Price rises 10% and quantity falls 20%. PED = −20 ÷ 10 = **−2**, so demand is **elastic**.\n\n## Revenue rule\n- If demand is **elastic**, a price cut **raises** revenue.\n- If demand is **inelastic**, a price rise **raises** revenue.\n\n> Tip: Necessities such as salt or electricity tend to be inelastic.\n\n> Try it: Price rises 5% and quantity falls 15%. What is the size of PED? || 3", {"title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"prompt": "A PED of 0.5 (ignoring sign) means demand is...", "options": ["Elastic", "Inelastic", "Unit elastic", "Perfectly elastic"], "answer": 1, "why": "Below 1 is inelastic."}, {"prompt": "Price up 5% and quantity down 15% gives a PED size of...", "options": ["0.33", "3", "10", "20"], "answer": 1, "why": "15 ÷ 5 = 3."}, {"prompt": "If demand is elastic, cutting the price will...", "options": ["Lower revenue", "Raise revenue", "Not change revenue", "Remove demand"], "answer": 1, "why": "Quantity rises by more than price falls."}]}],
      ]),
